---
// How Ikenga sits beside Claude Code's own organisation controls. Claude Code
// is named only to say what Ikenga works with. Engine names and maturity come
// from the engines data; an engine whose tier is hidden is never named.
import { enginesByTier, feature } from '../../lib/truth/read';

interface Props {
	/** The short form for /security: what still applies, and the MCP policy note. */
	short?: boolean;
}
const { short = false } = Astro.props;

// Each claim below has a released row behind it; a missing or unreleased row fails the build.
['engines.claude-code', 'chi.engines', 'workspace.frame', 'terminal.continuity', 'ngwa.store', 'ngwa.git-install', 'registry.signed-catalog', 'vault.passphrase', 'chi.seats'].forEach(
	(id) => feature(id),
);
// What Claude Code does further: Ikenga's matching items are not released yet.
const orgPolicy = feature('policy.org-apps', ['exploring', 'next', 'in-progress']);
const oidc = feature('access.oidc-sign-in', ['exploring', 'next', 'in-progress']);
const LANE: Record<string, string> = { exploring: 'we are exploring it', next: 'it is next on our roadmap', 'in-progress': 'it is in progress' };
const OIDC: Record<string, string> = {
	exploring: 'We are exploring sign-in with your identity provider.',
	next: 'Sign-in with your identity provider is next.',
	'in-progress': 'Sign-in with your identity provider is in progress.',
};

const WORD: Record<string, string> = { supported: 'supported', beta: 'in beta', experimental: 'experimental' };
const join = (names: string[]) =>
	names.length < 2 ? names.join('') : `${names.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and ${names[names.length - 1]}`;
const engineParts = enginesByTier().map((g) => `${join(g.names)} ${g.names.length > 1 ? 'are' : 'is'} ${WORD[g.tier]}`);
const engineSentence =
	engineParts.length < 2
		? `${engineParts.join('')}.`
		: `${engineParts.slice(0, -1).join('. ')}, and ${engineParts[engineParts.length - 1]}.`;
---

<p>
	Claude Code has its own controls for organisations. Your admins can deploy managed settings that local settings
	cannot override. They can decide which MCP servers may run, require single sign-on, and route usage through Amazon
	Bedrock, Google Cloud or Microsoft Foundry. Ikenga does not replace any of that, and it does not route around it.
	Ikenga runs your own Claude Code installation with your own sign-in, so the settings your organisation deploys still
	apply.
</p>
{
	!short && (
		<>
			<p>What Ikenga adds is the workspace around your agents:</p>
			<ul class="lane">
				<li>
					<strong>More than one engine.</strong> {engineSentence} Each uses your own install, sign-in or key.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A local-first workspace.</strong> Projects, the files your agents make, terminals and agent sessions
					are kept on your computer. Your engine still sends your prompts to its own model provider. Ikenga itself
					sends no usage analytics; the engines you run follow their own settings.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A store with consent.</strong> Claude Code has no built-in catalogue of MCP servers. Ngwa, the store,
					installs skills, tools, hooks and apps from a signed catalogue, where you see what each one can access before
					it installs. Skills, agents, commands, hooks and tools can also install from a public git source, pinned to
					the commit you reviewed.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A vault and seats.</strong> Keys stay in a passphrase vault. Named seats keep track of each
					project's agent sessions, with their cost and permission requests.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>Where Claude Code's controls go further today.</strong> They enforce policy on every machine in your
				organisation. Claude's business plans also add single sign-on, plus SCIM and audit logs on Enterprise. Ikenga
				has no organisation-wide policy for apps and tools yet; {LANE[orgPolicy.status]}. {OIDC[oidc.status]}
			</p>
		</>
	)
}
<p>
	Your MCP policy also covers Ikenga's tools. Ikenga adds the tools it installs to Claude Code's own configuration
	files, so your allowlists and denylists apply to them. If your admins deploy a fixed set of servers
	(<code>managed-mcp.json</code>), Claude Code does not load tools installed through Ikenga.
</p>
